What CC0 covers, and what it does not
-------------------------------------

CC0 is a waiver of copyright and related rights in a work. Here the work is the drawing:
the SVG path a contributor produced. CC0 section 4(a) is explicit that it does not reach
trademark:

    No trademark or patent rights held by Affirmer are waived, abandoned, surrendered,
    licensed or otherwise affected by this document.

That limit is not a technicality. The person who drew a brand's logo as an SVG is not the
person who owns the brand, so they were never in a position to waive anything on the brand
owner's behalf. Every mark here depicts a trademark that belongs to someone else. Copyright
in the drawing and trademark in the thing drawn are separate, and CC0 only speaks to the
first.

Trademark law generally turns on how a mark is used rather than on how a file was licensed:
who is displaying it, next to what, and whether an audience could read it as affiliation,
sponsorship, or endorsement. Displaying a logo to label a service a person already
subscribes to is a different use from putting the same logo on a marketing page. Working out
which uses are open to you is your job, and for anything commercial it is a question for a
lawyer rather than for a README.


What Simple Icons itself says
-----------------------------

The project publishes a disclaimer at DISCLAIMER.md in its repository. Its substance, in our
words:

  * Simple Icons being CC0 does not imply that every icon within the project is CC0.
    Individual icons may be offered under other licences.

  * The project records licence data per icon where it has it. That data is contributed and
    maintained by a community, so it can be incomplete or out of date. The absence of a
    licence for an icon does not mean the icon has no licence.

  * Licences change. Simple Icons asks users to check regularly whether the licence of an
    icon they use has changed.

  * Where a brand publishes branding guidelines, Simple Icons links to them and asks users
    to read them and keep their usage in line with them.

  * Simple Icons states that it cannot be held responsible for any legal activity raised by
    a brand or by users of the package, and asks that users seek the correct permissions for
    the icons relevant to their project.

  * Brands can ask for their icon to be removed, and icons are removed between releases.
    A slug that resolves today may be gone from the next release. Regenerating the catalogue
    can therefore drop marks, and `Scripts/generate_marks.py` reports every slug it could
    not find.

Read the disclaimer yourself rather than relying on this summary:

