Per icon licences in this catalogue
-----------------------------------

This package bundles every icon Simple Icons ships. Where Simple Icons records a licence for
one, the generator carries it into `BrandMarks.json` under a `license` key rather than
discarding it, and it is readable on the mark:

```swift
BundledCatalog.mark(slug: "anki")?.license   // BundledMarkLicense(type: "AGPL-3.0-only")
```

To see every mark that carries recorded terms, and what those terms are:

```swift
let licensed = BundledCatalog.all.compactMap { mark in
    mark.license.map { (mark.slug, $0.type, $0.url) }
}
```

In the generation currently checked in, 3453 marks are bundled. 223 of them carry recorded
licence data, and 212 of those are something other than CC0. The families present are
attribution (CC-BY), share alike (CC-BY-SA), permissive software licences (MIT, Apache-2.0,
BSD-3-Clause, MPL-2.0), copyleft (GPL, AGPL), and 23 marks under terms the brand sets
itself, each with a URL in the data. 72 marks carry copyleft or share alike terms.

Two groups deserve naming, because their terms conflict with uses people commonly assume are
open. 13 marks are under NonCommercial or NoDerivatives terms:

    cocoapods        CC-BY-NC-4.0
    letsencrypt      CC-BY-NC-4.0
    nodebb           CC-BY-NC-4.0
    rubocop          CC-BY-NC-4.0
    gamebanana       CC-BY-NC-ND-4.0
    pycqa            CC-BY-NC-ND-4.0
    tauri            CC-BY-NC-ND-4.0
    sass             CC-BY-NC-SA-3.0
    misskey          CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
    robotframework   CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
    vuedotjs         CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
    jpeg             CC-BY-ND-4.0
    slint            CC-BY-ND-4.0

NonCommercial terms speak to whether the work may be used commercially. NoDerivatives terms
speak to whether it may be modified, and this package does modify: it parses the path and
rescales it into whatever rectangle the caller asks for. If you are shipping a commercial
product, or if either of those matters to you, these are the entries to look at first.

The counts and lists above describe one generation of the file at one point in time. They are
not a complete account of the terms attached to every mark, because Simple Icons has licence
data for only a fraction of its icons and because that data changes between releases. An
entry with no licence is not thereby CC0; it means nothing is on file. Check the JSON you
actually ship.


